    "#21.Why is NumPy’s array slicing faster than Python’s list slicing?\n",
    "'''\n",
    "Contiguous Memory Storage – NumPy arrays store data in a continuous block of memory, unlike \n",
    "Python lists, which are collections of pointers.\n",
    "\n",
    "Vectorized Operations – Slicing in NumPy does not create copies by default; it returns a view of \n",
    "the original array, reducing memory and computation time.\n",
    "\n",
    "Optimized C Backend – NumPy operations are implemented in compiled C code, making slicing and \n",
    "computations much faster.\n",
    "\n",
    "Fixed Data Type – All elements in a NumPy array have the same type, allowing the CPU to process\n",
    "elements efficiently.\n",
    "\n",
    "Reduced Overhead – No per-element type checking is needed, unlike Python lists which can hold mixed\n",
    "types.\n",
    "\n",
    "'''"
   ]
